<header class="soc-head"><p class="featured-region">Social ladder · 1858–1947 (Crown rule in India, around 1900)</p><h1 class="soc-title">Social Hierarchy of British India</h1><p class="soc-sub">From Viceroy to Indentured Labourer</p></header>
<div class="soc-intro"><figure class="soc-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar.jpg" alt="Delhi Durbar"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar">Delhi Durbar</a><span class="soc-credit">Unknown photographer · Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p class="soc-lede"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> was ranked by race and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> as well as by wealth and office. The viceroy governed in the Crown’s name, and a little over a thousand men of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>, almost all of them British, ran the districts; Indians held the lower posts. Nearly 600 princes ruled their states under British paramountcy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> held the great estates of the north and east: both were rewarded for keeping out of the rebellion of 1857. Colonial rule also made caste a central mechanism of administration. From 1881 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> ranked thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jati">jatis</a> by their supposed purity, administrative jobs went only to the upper castes, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criminal-tribes-act">Criminal Tribes Act</a> of 1871 declared whole communities criminal by birth. Most Indians were peasants, who paid the land tax on which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a>’s finances rested and died in the tens of millions in the famines of the later 19th century; the communities treated as untouchable stood outside the caste order altogether, and more than 1.6 million Indians were shipped overseas as indentured labourers.</p><p class="soc-facts"><span><b>Period</b> 1858–1947 (Crown rule in India, around 1900)</span><span><b>People</b> c. 250–300 million (est.)</span><span><b>Ranks</b> 12</span><span><b>Profile</b> <a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj</a></span></p></div>
<section class="soc-ladder-sec" aria-labelledby="soc-ladder-t"><div class="soc-ladder-head"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-ladder-t">The ladder</h2><div class="soc-scale" role="group" aria-label="How to draw the ladder"><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="0" aria-pressed="true">By rank</button><button type="button" class="soc-scale-btn" data-scale="1" aria-pressed="false">To scale</button></div></div><ol class="soc-pyr" data-scale="0"><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:24%;--k:0"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-ruler" href="#rank-1" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Viceroy</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">One ruler</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:39%;--k:1"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility" href="#rank-2" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">British officials and residents</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">Fewer than 1 in 1,000</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-3" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">The princes</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">Fewer than 1 in 1,000 (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:54%;--k:2"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-nobility soc-est" href="#rank-4" style="--sw:2%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Zamindar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Zamindars and great landholders)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 1–2% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-religious soc-est" href="#rank-5" style="--sw:6%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Brahmin</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 4–7% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:70%;--k:3"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-6" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">The educated middle class</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–1% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-specialists soc-est" href="#rank-7" style="--sw:3%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Merchants, bankers and moneylenders</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 2–4% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:85%;--k:4"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-8" style="--sw:63%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Ryot</span> <span class="soc-en">(Peasants)</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">About 63% (the rest, est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-commoners soc-est" href="#rank-9" style="--sw:10%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Artisans, mill hands and labourers</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 8–12% (est.)</span></a></li><li class="soc-row" style="--rw:100%;--k:5"><a class="soc-bar soc-b-outcast soc-est" href="#rank-10" style="--sw:15%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Castes treated as untouchable</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 12–18% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-outcast soc-est" href="#rank-11" style="--sw:1%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Castes and tribes declared criminal</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></a><a class="soc-bar soc-b-dependent soc-est" href="#rank-12" style="--sw:0.4%"><span class="soc-bar-n"><span class="soc-loc">Indentured labourers</span></span><span class="soc-bar-s">Fewer than 1 in 1,000 (est.)</span></a></li></ol><p class="soc-scale-note">By rank, the ladder is drawn as a pyramid. To scale, each bar is as wide as that rank&#8217;s share of the people. Shares marked est. are estimates where no count survives; their boxes and bars are dashed.</p></section>
<button type="button" class="kd-story soc-kd-open">Read the whole story</button>
<section class="soc-ranks" aria-label="The ranks, from the top"><article class="soc-rank soc-b-ruler" id="rank-1"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/george-curzon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Ruler</span> · <span>One ruler</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Viceroy</span></h3><p class="soc-term"><span class="soc-nw">governor-general</span></p><p class="soc-kid">The viceroy ruled India for the British queen or king, who lived far away in Britain.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">After 1858 the governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was more commonly called the viceroy, from his second role as the Crown’s representative to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>. Appointed by the Crown, he headed the Government of India from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> with an Executive Council whose members each ran a department, and he answered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and through him to Parliament. Laws made in India needed the Secretary of State’s assent, which led Sir Charles Wood to call the Government of India “a despotism controlled from home”. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a> (1899–1905) reformed everything from irrigation and the police to the universities and in 1905 divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. In 1911 a move of the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> was announced.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> The Crown appointed him, for no fixed term though typically for five years. Curzon resigned in 1905 after a dispute with his military chief, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Lord Kitchener</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> One man, appointed by the Crown, typically for five years; the monarch, proclaimed Empress or Emperor of India, remained in Britain.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">George Curzon</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Louis Mountbatten</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy of India</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar">Delhi Durbar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-2"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/british-raj-2.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>Fewer than 1 in 1,000</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">British officials and residents</span></h3><p class="soc-term">Civilians, covenanted civil service</p><p class="soc-kid">A small group of British officials, chosen by exams held in London, ran the districts of British India.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">British control in the districts rested on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a> (ICS), whose members, the “Civilians”, were at first almost all British and educated in the best British schools. A little over a thousand of them oversaw all government in some 250 districts, each with an average of 300,000 subjects. They entered by a competitive examination, open from 1853 to British subjects without distinction of race but held only in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The higher, covenanted service was British; the uncovenanted service let Indians in at the lower rungs. Early in the 20th century 8,000 European officials earned £13.9 million between them, and 130,000 Indians in government service £3.3 million. Until the 1930s the railways hired only European supervisors and engineers.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A candidate had to be aged 18 to 23 and pass a horse-riding test; after probation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, an Assistant Commissioner began on about £300 a year, served at least 25 years and retired on a pension of £1,000. The examination was at first held only in London; from 1922 Indian candidates could sit it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Roughly: the census of 1861 counted 125,945 English people in India, about 84,000 of them officers and men of the army and some 42,000 civilians; against the 255 million of the census of 1881, about 0.05 per cent. The Indian Civil Service itself was a little over a thousand men.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: The British Residency at Hyderabad, about 1900 · Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_residence%2C_Hyderabad.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-3"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>Fewer than 1 in 1,000 (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">The princes</span></h3><p class="soc-term">maharaja, raja, nawab, nizam</p><p class="soc-kid">Indian princes still ruled hundreds of states, big and small, but Britain controlled their armies and their dealings with other countries.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Nearly 600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, covering 40 per cent of the land and 23 per cent of its people in 1947, were ruled by Indian princes under British paramountcy. By treaty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> controlled their external affairs, defence and most communications, and a British resident or agent watched over them; the princes could not deal with foreign states or fight one another, and their armies served for ceremony and policing. The British translated every title, maharaja, nawab or nizam, as “prince”, so that no ruler seemed a king equal to the British monarch, and ranked the rulers by gun salutes, from 3 guns to 21. Many states were tiny: some two hundred covered less than 25 square kilometres.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A prince inherited his throne, but the British could grant or withhold recognition. The doctrine of lapse, under which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> had annexed states whose ruler died without a natural heir, ended in 1858. Princes who had kept out of the rebellion of 1857 had their territories guaranteed, and their sons were schooled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayo-college">Mayo College</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aitchison-college">Aitchison College</a> on the model of the English public schools.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the ruling houses of nearly 600 states (565 were recognised in 1947) with their families; the Imperial Gazetteer counted some 2,000 ruling chiefs with the title raja or maharaja.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii">Sayajirao Gaekwad III</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhupinder-singh-of-patiala">Bhupinder Singh of Patiala</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukoji-rao-holkar-iii">Tukoji Rao Holkar III</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">Princely state</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayo-college">Mayo College</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aitchison-college">Aitchison College</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doctrine-of-lapse">Doctrine of lapse</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-nobility" id="rank-4"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zamindar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Nobility and officials</span> · <span>c. 1–2% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Zamindar</span> <span class="soc-en">(Zamindars and great landholders)</span></h3><p class="soc-term">zamindari</p><p class="soc-kid">Zamindars were rich landowners who paid the government a fixed sum each year for their great estates.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> held the great estates of the north and east. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> zamindars had not owned the land, only the right to collect its revenue, but in 1793 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/permanent-settlement">Permanent Settlement</a> made the zamindars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> proprietors of their land in return for a fixed annual rent. If a zamindar could not pay by sunset, parts of his estate were auctioned, which created a new class of zamindars. After 1857 their armies were abolished, save a few forces for policing. Most of the great zamindars were of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhumihar">Bhumihar</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayastha">Kayastha</a>, and the British gave loyal ones titles such as Raja, Rai Bahadur, Nawab or Khan Bahadur.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> An estate passed by descent or adoption, and a lawfully wedded wife could inherit it if the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindar</a> named her heir; the British rewarded some supportive zamindars by recognising them as princes. Because many peasants had fought for their former landlords in 1857, the British made no more land reforms for 90 years, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> remained realms of large landholdings.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the zamindars, great and small, with their families; the articles give no count. The Imperial Gazetteer reckoned some 2,000 ruling chiefs with royal titles and ten times as many holders of lesser noble titles, and the princely states held thousands of zamindari estates and jagirs besides.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">Zamindar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">Jagir</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhumihar">Bhumihar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayastha">Kayastha</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/permanent-settlement">Permanent Settlement</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-religious" id="rank-5"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/brahmins.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Religious and learned</span> · <span>c. 4–7% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Brahmin</span></h3><p class="soc-term">varna</p><p class="soc-kid">Brahmins were the priestly caste, ranked highest in old Hindu writings, and the British gave government jobs to the upper castes.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> scheme of the ancient texts placed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, the priests, at the head of four orders, above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriyas</a> (rulers and warriors), the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishya">Vaishyas</a> (traders) and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a> (labourers), with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalits</a> and Adivasis outside. Scholars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> call it “a social ideal rather than a social reality”, but colonial rule made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> central to administration. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> rule, Brahmins became the authorities on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> law; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> from 1881 ranked thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jati">jatis</a> by supposed purity; and between 1860 and 1920 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> gave administrative jobs and senior appointments only to the upper castes. After 1857 the Brahmin and Muslim units of the United Provinces, the core of the rebellion, were disbanded.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Birth into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jati">jati</a> decided it. Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> sought Brahmin or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> status for whole communities, a process called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskritisation">Sanskritisation</a>; the upper castes had resisted such claims before British rule, but they spread under it.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the articles give no count for the period; at the present day the forward castes, of whom the Brahmins are one, are estimated at 26 per cent of India’s Hindus.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudra</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskritisation">Sanskritisation</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-6"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bal-gangadhar-tilak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 0.5–1% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">The educated middle class</span></h3><p class="soc-term">bhadralok (Bengal)</p><p class="soc-kid">Indians who studied at the new universities became lawyers and officials, and some started the Indian National Congress.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">By 1880 a new middle class had arisen across the country. The universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> opened in 1857, and by 1890 some 60,000 Indians had matriculated, chiefly in arts or law; a third entered public administration and another third became lawyers. In 1887 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims held 52 per cent of 21,000 mid-level civil service posts, but almost none of the thousand at the top. Racial discrimination angered them, and the partial reversal in 1883 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilbert-bill">Ilbert Bill</a>, which would have put Indian judges in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> on an equal footing with British ones, turned discontent into politics: in December 1885 seventy of them, many familiar with British political thought, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Education was the way in: matriculation, a degree in arts or law, and a post in the administration or at the bar. Few climbed higher, since almost all the top posts went to Britons, typically with an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> degree.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: some 60,000 Indians had matriculated by 1890, and in 1911 the colleges enrolled 36,000 students; with the clerks, teachers and lawyers of the towns and their families, perhaps one person in a hundred or two hundred.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Bal Gangadhar Tilak</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilbert-bill">Ilbert Bill</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servants-of-india-society">Servants of India Society</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-specialists" id="rank-7"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/british-raj-7.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Soldiers, merchants and craftsmen</span> · <span>c. 2–4% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Merchants, bankers and moneylenders</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Merchants and bankers traded and lent money, and some Indians built cotton mills and a great steelworks.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a> scheme trade belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishya">Vaishyas</a>. As railways carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and grain to the ports for export, many small farmers dependent on faraway markets lost their land, animals and equipment to moneylenders. Indians also built industry: in 1877 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamsetji-tata">Jamsetji Tata</a> began a spinning and weaving company in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> whose finer yarn, spun from Egyptian cotton on American ring spindles, could compete with British imports, and in 1908 his son’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tata-steel">Tata Iron and Steel Company</a> began a steelworks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamshedpur">Jamshedpur</a>, its surplus <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> promised a buyer in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a>. Yet the government’s Stores Policy shut most Indian firms out of railway contracts. Most big businessmen were nationalists who distrusted the Congress as too socialist.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Wealth could buy land, but in the north-west the Land Alienation Act of 1900 listed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> that could own land and barred its transfer to non-agricultural castes. The reforms of 1909 favoured rich landowners and businessmen, and those of 1919 reserved seats for businessmen.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the articles give no count; taken here at about the share estimated for the merchants and bankers of Mughal India.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamsetji-tata">Jamsetji Tata</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishya">Vaishya</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tata-steel">Tata Steel</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamshedpur">Jamshedpur</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Marwari merchants in Delhi, 1868 · New York Public Library · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marwarrees%2C_Hindoo_merchants%2C_originally_from_Marwar%2C_Delhi_(NYPL_b13409080-1125440).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-8"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/british-raj-8.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>About 63% (the rest, est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Ryot</span> <span class="soc-en">(Peasants)</span></h3><p class="soc-kid">Most Indians were farmers who paid taxes on their land, and millions died when terrible famines came.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Most Indians were peasants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryot">ryots</a>, living at subsistence level. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a>’s finances rested on the land tax, collected through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindars</a> in the north and, in the south, under the ryotwari system, from cultivators the government treated as landowners. Taxpayers, chiefly farmers and farm labourers, also bore the railways’ £50 million of risk. Many small farmers lost their land to moneylenders, and great famines struck: the Great Famine of 1876–1878 killed 6.1 to 10.39 million people while, as Mike Davis notes, wheat exports to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> went on unchecked, and the famine of 1899–1900 between 1.25 and 10 million. From 1880 the Famine Codes set out relief; in the newly irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> a more prosperous peasantry grew.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Peasant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> tried to rise as groups: under British rule pastoral communities such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahir">Ahirs</a> took the name Yadav, and tilling castes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurmi">Kurmis</a> formed caste associations claiming higher status. In 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> took up the cause of tenant farmers in Champaran, forced to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> for British planters.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Everyone else: the peasants who tilled the land, with their families, the great majority of the people. The other shares are estimates, so this one is too.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryot">Ryot</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/famine">Famine</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryotwari">Ryotwari</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurmi">Kurmi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahir">Ahir</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A Kunbi cultivator of the Konkan, a watercolour by Frank James, about 1870 · Captain Cornelius Francis (Frank) James · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:'Concan_Coonbie'_cultivator%2C_India%2C_1870_(c).jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-commoners" id="rank-9"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/british-raj-9.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Free commoners</span> · <span>c. 8–12% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Artisans, mill hands and labourers</span></h3><p class="soc-term">-</p><p class="soc-kid">Many weavers lost their work to cheap British cloth, while others worked in new cotton mills and on the railways.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Cheap British cloth, imported under the “free trade” <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> imposed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, shrank its largest industry: imports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth rose from about a million yards in 1814 to 2,050 million in 1890, and by 1870–1880 Indian producers made only 25 to 45 per cent of the cloth India used. The iron industry declined still further. New work came in the mills, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> plantations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and on the railways, where hard physical labour was left to Indians and their deaths, the historian Headrick shows, were “either ignored or merely mentioned as a cold statistical figure”. In 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> fasted to support low-paid workers striking at an Indian-owned mill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmedabad">Ahmedabad</a>, which won a settlement.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Weavers and ironworkers lost their trades to imports, while others found work in the mills and on the railways, where Indians were kept to unskilled work: in 1920 they held only ten per cent of the railways’ superior posts.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the articles give no count; the weavers and other craftspeople, the mill, railway and plantation workers and the labourers of the towns with their families, taken as about one in ten.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">Tea</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Labourers carrying baskets of cotton to the mills, Indore, about 1900 · Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coolies_carrying_baskets_of_cotton_from_huge_dump_to_the_mills.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-outcast" id="rank-10"><a class="soc-rank-pic" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/b-r-ambedkar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></a><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Outcast groups</span> · <span>c. 12–18% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Castes treated as untouchable</span></h3><p class="soc-term">untouchables, Depressed Classes</p><p class="soc-kid">People of the castes called untouchable were kept apart, even at the village well, and reformers like Ambedkar fought for their rights.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">Outside the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a> stood the communities treated as untouchable, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalits</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a> rules held them apart: the dominant caste lived at the centre of a village and others on its edges, there were minute rules on the food and drink the upper castes would accept from lower castes, and a caste considered impure might not draw water from a well used by others. By the late 19th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criminal-tribes-act">Criminal Tribes Act</a> reached most untouchable castes, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamar">Chamars</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jyotirao-phule">Jyotirao Phule</a> rejected every claim that caste was natural or ordained by the Creator, and Gokhale’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servants-of-india-society">Servants of India Society</a> worked among them. From the 1920s the government reserved a share of its jobs for the lower castes.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Birth decided it, and conversion did not undo it: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> the Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalit</a> Christians of the missions were still treated as lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>, born into a caste classified as untouchable, became a leader of human-rights campaigns; at his request the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communal-award">Communal Award</a> of 1932 gave the depressed classes separate electorates, which he gave up in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>’s hunger strike.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the articles give no count for the period; in 2001 the Scheduled Castes, the communities once treated as untouchable, were 16.2 per cent of India’s population.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jati">Jati</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communal-award">Communal Award</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalit</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamar">Chamar</a></div></div></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-outcast" id="rank-11"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/british-raj-11.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Outcast groups</span> · <span>c. 0.5–2% (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Castes and tribes declared criminal</span></h3><p class="soc-term">criminal tribes</p><p class="soc-kid">A British law of 1871 labelled whole castes as born criminals and limited where their members could go.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criminal-tribes-act">Criminal Tribes Act</a> of 1871 declared that all who belonged to certain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> were born with criminal tendencies. It first covered castes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahir">Ahirs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurjars">Gurjars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a>, and by the late 19th century its enforcement reached most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudras</a> and untouchables, Sannyasis and hill tribes. Castes suspected of rebellion, such as the Kallars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravar">Maravars</a> of the south, once ruling families, were called “predatory and barbarian”, some with no record of crime, only forefathers who had risen against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> or the British. Registered members were restricted in where they could go and with whom they could mix; in some regions whole caste groups were arrested, their children taken from them, and held in penal colonies without conviction.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> Birth and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> register decided it. The practice was disputed among British officials, and in a few cases it was reversed early in the 20th century on the ground that people “could not be incarcerated indefinitely on the presumption of [inherited] bad character”; elsewhere the lists grew through the 1930s.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: the articles give no count; hundreds of communities came under the Criminal Tribes Act, 237 castes and tribes in the Madras Presidency alone by 1931.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jats">Jats</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurjars">Gurjars</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maravar">Maravar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criminal-tribes-act">Criminal Tribes Act</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: A government pamphlet on a &quot;gang&quot; under the Criminal Tribes Act, 1942 · Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Pamphlet_on_Gobinda_Doms_Gang%2C_under_the_Criminal_Tribes_Act_(VI_of_1924)%2C_dated_1942.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article><article class="soc-rank soc-b-dependent" id="rank-12"><span class="soc-rank-pic" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/societies/thumbs/british-raj-12.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="88" height="88"></span><div class="soc-rank-b"><p class="soc-rank-k"><span>Serfs and dependants</span> · <span>Fewer than 1 in 1,000 (est.)</span></p><h3 class="soc-rank-t"><span class="soc-loc">Indentured labourers</span></h3><p class="soc-term">girmit</p><p class="soc-kid">Many poor Indians were sent to work on sugar farms on faraway islands, often after being tricked by recruiters.</p><div class="soc-more"><p class="soc-text">From 1834, when the ship Atlas carried 36 labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, more than 1.6 million Indians were shipped to European colonies whose planters wanted cheap labour after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>. Officials called it “free” migration, but many recruits were deceived, coerced or kidnapped. A labourer bound himself for five years: under the agreement of 1912 he worked nine hours a day, five days a week, and five on Saturdays, cutting cane and digging channels, for a shilling a day, a woman for nine pence. In British Guiana absence from work was a crime punishable by prison, and overseers flogged and beat. Historians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-tinker">Hugh Tinker</a> call it “a new form of slavery”.</p><p class="soc-rise"><b>Moving in or out.</b> A recruit had to appear before a magistrate of his district, and a free passage home came only after ten years’ residence. Labourers resisted by slowdowns, strikes, sabotage and desertion; many stayed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> gave five acres and £5 to those who gave up their passage. Indenture to Natal ended in 1911, after Gokhale’s bill, and to the other colonies in 1917.</p><p class="soc-sn"><b>How many?</b> Estimate: they lived overseas, outside India’s population; 525,482 Indians emigrated to British and French colonies between 1842 and 1870, some 19,000 a year on five-year contracts, so perhaps 100,000 were under indenture at any one time, about 0.04 per cent of India’s people. More than 1.6 million went in all.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar plantation</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-indenture-system">Indian indenture system</a></div></div><p class="soc-pic-cr">Picture: Indian indentured labourers on a sugar estate in Mauritius, 1870 · Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_indentured_labourers_on_a_sugar_estate%2C_Mauritius%2C_1870.jpg" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></p></div></article></section>
<section class="soc-born" id="born" aria-labelledby="soc-born-t"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="soc-born-t">Born into history</h2><p class="soc-born-lede">Where would you have stood? Chance places you on the ladder, as birth placed most people: weighted by how many each rank held.</p><button type="button" class="soc-born-btn" data-born="british-raj">Be born in British India</button><div class="soc-born-out" aria-live="polite"></div></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Moving up and down</h2><p>Historians such as Nicholas Dirks argue that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> affiliation was loose and fluid before colonial rule and that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> enforced it rigorously, building a stricter hierarchy than before; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a> of 1901 and 1911 hardened caste identities, and jobs, schooling and the right to own land were allotted by caste. Communities answered by organising: caste associations, a by-product of the census, petitioned for a higher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurmi">Kurmis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahir">Ahirs</a>, Jatavs and others claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> status. Education opened a narrow door into the middle class and the lower civil service, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a> slowly admitted Indians: from 1922 they could sit its examination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, the Lee Commission of 1924 set future intakes at 40 per cent Europeans and 40 per cent Indians, the rest promoted from the provincial services, and by 1945 Indians outnumbered Britons in the service. Land changed hands too: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindari</a> estates were auctioned for unpaid rent and small farmers lost their land to moneylenders, while in the north-west the Land Alienation Act of 1900 barred its transfer to non-agricultural castes. From the 1920s the government reserved a share of its jobs for the lower castes. For the poorest, indenture overseas was a way out with a heavy price; many stayed, and their descendants are now about two-thirds of the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>.</p></section>
<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Women on the ladder</h2><p>No woman was ever recruited into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>; women worked in government only as typists and clerks in the lower ranks of the uncovenanted service, and over 90 per cent of the 36,000 college students of 1911 were men. An ICS officer’s widow drew £300 a year, hence the saying that an ICS marriage was worth “three hundred a year alive or dead”. After 1857 the British avoided intervening in religion, even over the remarriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> child widows, though sati had been banned before the rebellion; Indian reformers took up the cause, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandita-ramabai">Pandita Ramabai</a>, who championed the remarriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> widows, to Gokhale’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servants-of-india-society">Servants of India Society</a>. Ambedkar argued that sati, enforced widowhood and child marriage had grown up to reinforce marriage within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindar</a>’s lawfully wedded wife could inherit the estate if he named her heir, and Travancore had queens regent. Indentured women were paid nine pence to a man’s shilling, worked in the fields as well as at home, and suffered widespread sexual exploitation; on early ships there might be one woman to forty men, and from 1868 the rules required 40 women to every 100 men.</p></section>
